				subtitle: 'Programming of architecture',
				abstract: [
					'Unlike Newtonian cosmology, cybernetics views the world as a pattern, rather than a sum of matter. The pattern is a reflection of the interactions between and within the individual parts. From the human nervous system to a computer, and from a single-family accommodation to a city, the school of cybernetics suggests that such organisations can be programmed; it is therefore possible to yield desired outputs by manipulating the patterns. Architecture, in this process, plays a vital role as both a vehicle for social agendas and an instrument of social engineering. In an effort to mobilise all disposable resources for the Cold War – especially during a time when neither side could afford mutual assured destruction – housing and infrastructure projects became the main stage for ideological confrontation. Among them, those built in remote and isolated places, namely the polar regions and outer space, best represent the practice of programming and the cultures inscribed in the programs.',
					'This text explores the mechanisms of cybernetics in architecture: specifically, the social and human engineering of polar and space architectures. As I will demonstrate, architectural programming in polar regions and space began in 1957 with the opening of the International Geophysical Year. The topic of cybernetics and its application in architecture has been intensively investigated in recent years. However, polar and space architectures remained largely isolated from the discussions. This text will review Friederich Kittler’s article “The City is a Medium” as a point of departure. Furthermore, the text will examine cybernetic theories from an architectural perspective and introduce polar and space architectures to investigate cybernetic mechanisms. In doing so, I wish to establish a methodological framework for my future case studies. With ongoing climate change and increasing geopolitical unrest in the polar regions, this article aims to offer a new reading of the architectural history and theory of the North and South Poles, as well as space. It should be noted that, although the term “polar architecture” is highly debatable due to its geopolitical nature, I will continue to use it only as a geographical concept. The term will be further examined at the end of the text in terms of geopolitical and cultural implications.',
