				abstract: [
					'“A great empire, like a great cake, is most easily diminished at the edges.”  Despite the distance from industrial societies, habitation of the polar regions began to take on a different significance after the Second World War. This irreversible change took place in both aspects of technology and geopolitics. The British Empire, as one of the old powers, struggled to maintain its control in the Arctic and Antarctic waters against the military challenges from Germany. With the advancement of transportation and communication technologies, new expeditioners were better supplied than Ernest Shackleton and Robert Scott, who were supported by the Arctic convoys and Operation Tabarin. More importantly, polar exploration no longer remained a public recreation and personal enterprise but became a part of national strategy and an icon of strength. The edge of the cake known as the Earth – the Arctic and Antarctic – thus became new areas to be contested by the old and new global powers.'
