				abstract: [
					'On June 14, 1999, a list titled “The 100 Worst Ideas of the Century” was printed in Time magazine. On page 37, positioned in the middle of that list, there was Biosphere 2.  Although without a rank, the world’s first large-scale artificial biosphere was placed after “DDT,” “Staffing the White House with Interns During the Government Shutdown,” and “Hydrogen-Filled Blimps.”',
					'The Biosphere 2 laboratory was a materially closed artificial biosphere that first entered operation in 1991. In addition to its controversies, it was known for its unprecedented scales, space colonialisation rhetoric, and promises of scientific discovery. The media coverage has mainly portrayed the Biosphere 2 laboratory as a research institution since its early operations, yet this perspective captures only one of its many roles. Regrettably, however, its cultural and theoretical ties with the Countercultural Movement are less often visited. Most importantly, the architectural analysis of the structure remains marginal. While providing an overview of the Biosphere 2 laboratory’s history, this article examines the design from multiple perspectives including architecture, science, culture, and finance. Biosphere 2’s controversies are deeply rooted in its struggle of prioritising multiple missions. This research shows that the conflicting missions are manifestations of the clash between different cultures: the subjective consciousness promoted during the Countercultural Movement and the rigid, rational reasoning of science, finance, and engineering.',
					'Instead of evaluating the success or failure of the project, this article is an architectural presentation of the site conditions that created the project yet complicated it in later years. Biosphere 2 remains referential today for architectural theory and design. It demonstrated the creation and maintenance of a world analogue. Biosphere 2 is not a mirror of the world outside, yet the difference stands the key to understand the attempt of defining and channelling the natural state, which is perhaps a constant theme of architectural exploration but usually obscured by other vocabularies and concepts. Furthermoe, Biosphere 2 provided valuable experiences in designing the bioregenerative life-sustaining system; these practical lessons can be applied to the future space and polar habitation that aim for longer missions.',
